{
  "teamId": 136,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Seattle Mariners were a team of tantalizing promise and quiet frustration, a club whose elite pitching and occasional offensive explosions kept them in the race for months before the weight of their offensive limitations finally dragged them under. Opening the year with a solid April that established genuine hope, Seattle rode its shutdown rotation and the emerging star power of Cal Raleigh into a productive spring, looking every bit like a playoff contender in the American League West.\n\nMay and June brought more of the same competitive baseball, even as the bullpen occasionally unraveled at the worst moments. Raleigh continued his assault on American League pitching, smashing grand slams and clutch home runs, while Julio Rodríguez flashed the talent that made him one of baseball's most exciting young players. The Mariners entered summer above .500 and in the thick of the wild card conversation, their 3.49 ERA serving as a foundation that most teams could only dream of building upon.\n\nJuly changed everything. The offense, never truly reliable behind a .687 OPS, grew ice cold at the worst possible time, and the bullpen — so often a strength — began surrendering leads with alarming regularity. Austin Voth and others were touched up for critical home runs, and the Mariners went 10-14 in July before a similarly disappointing August. Andrés Muñoz, one of the game's best closers, suffered through a brutal stretch of blown saves that cost Seattle dearly in the standings, a cruel irony for a team whose identity was built on pitching.\n\nSeptember offered one final push, and the Mariners answered the bell with a 16-10 record that was ultimately too little, too late. Julio Rodríguez delivered a stunning three-run homer on September 13th that captured everything this team could be, but a stunning strikeout-pickoff double play on the 18th and more late bullpen meltdowns epitomized the season's recurring heartbreak. A walk-off Shea Langeliers three-run blast on the final weekend in Oakland all but closed the casket on playoff hopes, and Seattle finished 85-77 — talented enough to compete, but not resilient enough to reach October.",
